{"author":"RebarBotNava","author_email":"joeoakhart+bot@navapbc.com","author_id":"594c-9dcf-5ad6-4e6d","author_sig":"{\"payload\": \"\", \"payloadType\": \"application/vnd.rebar.authorship.v1+json\", \"signatures\": [{\"keyid\": \"594c-9dcf-5ad6-4e6d\", \"sig\": \"\"}]}","data":{"body":"═══ OSS COMPARISON REFUTES MY OWN JUSTIFICATION, and exposes a SECOND silent drop still present in\nthe first version of this fix. Reworked. ═══\n\nResearched at the operator's request: how do popular, actively-maintained multi-provider projects\ndecide \"is this string already provider-qualified?\"\n\n**1. REGISTRY MEMBERSHIP IS THE NORM. No project uses a shape/pattern rule.** LangChain\n(`prefix in _BUILTIN_PROVIDERS`), LiteLLM (`prefix in litellm.provider_list`), pydantic-ai\n(`infer_provider_class` raises on an unknown provider), Vercel AI SDK (`NoSuchProviderError` listing\n`availableProviders`), simonw/llm (flat exact-match registry). So my deviation has no precedent.\n\n**2. MY STATED REASON FOR DEVIATING IS ANSWERABLE, and three projects show how.** I argued the\nregistry was unreachable at config-resolution time without importing the optional `[agents]` extra.\nBut LangChain's `_BUILTIN_PROVIDERS` is a module-level dict of provider name → LAZY IMPORT\nCOORDINATES (`\"bedrock\": (\"langchain_aws\", \"ChatBedrock\", ...)`) — consulting the NAME SET imports\nnothing. LiteLLM's `provider_list` is explicitly lazy-loaded with the comment \"to avoid importing\nLlmProviders at import time\" — the same constraint I have, already solved. The design move is to\nseparate \"which names are providers\" (a static string set) from \"what implements them\" (downstream).\nSo the objection was mine, not the architecture's.\n\n**3. THE BIGGEST FINDING — the real fix is GUARD ORDER, and I had not implemented it.** LangChain\nuses `:` as its separator AND is immune to this bug, because of the FIRST condition in its test:\n\n    if (not model_provider                                      # <-- explicit provider WINS\n        and \":\" in model\n        and model.split(\":\", 1)[0] in _BUILTIN_PROVIDERS): ...\n\nAn explicitly configured provider short-circuits the scan entirely, so no punctuation in a model id\ncan overrule it. **My first version still scanned first**, and I confirmed the resulting hole by\nexperiment:\n\n    _resolve_target(\"openai:gpt-4o\", provider=\"bedrock\")  ->  'openai:gpt-4o'\n\nThe configured `provider = \"bedrock\"` is STILL SILENTLY DISCARDED. That is the SAME DEFECT CLASS this\nticket is about — dropping an explicit instruction — merely reached by a different input. Fixing the\ncolon test alone left half the bug standing.\n\n**REWORKED.** `_resolve_target` now checks a configured provider FIRST: matching inline qualifier →\nunchanged (no double-prefix); CONFLICTING qualifier → `LLMConfigError` naming both providers; no\nqualifier → prefix it. Only when nothing is configured does it fall through to the qualifier scan and\nthen inference. Three new tests, including the control that agreement stays silent. 24/24 green; 652\npassed across the provider/runner/workflow areas; lint and typecheck clean.\n\nChose to RAISE on a contradiction rather than pick a winner: either choice discards an explicit\ninstruction, which is exactly what this ticket exists to stop. It is also consistent with the\nfail-fast direction already recorded on this ticket, and with prevailing practice — no researched\nproject silently drops a configured provider, and LiteLLM goes further, ASSERTING the configured\nprovider onto the string (`if custom_llm_provider and model.split(\"/\")[0] != custom_llm_provider`).\n\n**4. SEPARATOR: keep `:`.** pydantic-ai's own `KnownModelName` contains TWO-COLON entries\n(`'bedrock:anthropic.claude-haiku-4-5-20251001-v1:0'`), so \"provider, first colon, then a model name\nthat may itself contain colons\" is exactly our runtime's grammar — we are compatible. `/` (LiteLLM,\nOpenRouter) is collision-free for Bedrock and is arguably the better choice, but no project was found\nregretting or migrating a separator, and changing ours would be a breaking config change for no\ncorrectness gain now that the guard order is right.\n\n**5. STILL OUTSTANDING, and now better motivated than when I first deferred it.** The shape test\nremains in place for the no-configured-provider path, and it cannot produce the diagnostic a registry\ncan: a typo'd provider still passes silently —\n`_resolve_target(\"claude-opus-4-8\", \"bedrok\") -> 'bedrok:claude-opus-4-8'` — where a registry would\nraise \"unknown provider 'bedrok'; valid: [...]\". Switching that path to registry membership carries\nreal regression risk (an identifier-shaped provider valid in pydantic-ai but absent from our static\nlist would start being treated as unqualified and double-prefixed), so it wants its own ticket and\nits own curated list rather than a same-change edit. Filing that separately.\n\nA third option worth recording because our own runtime took it: pydantic-ai REMOVED bare-name\ninference in v2.0.0b1 — `Agent('gpt-5')` now raises and you must write `openai:gpt-5`. If rebar\nrequired explicit qualification the ambiguity would not exist at all. That is the cleanest end state\nand the natural direction of travel, at the cost of a breaking config change."},"env_id":"ade0f2ef-51cc-45a2-bb97-c09846cd3df5","event_type":"COMMENT","timestamp":1785628585228981001,"uuid":"ffe64973-6e26-4176-bc49-c40c0e8fbf5f"}